{
  "beruf": "pflege",
  "title": "Care work: older people at home and in care homes",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "portuguese-for-work run, 2026-11-22: written by hand for Portugal (European Portuguese, pt-PT), not yet read by a human. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation (the 'German' tab). Words already in the lexicon, the IMT deck or another sector are refs, with this job's own example and sense. Working conditions, contracts and rules are general orientation only.",
  "intro": "Caring for older people is one of the most common first jobs for newcomers in Portugal: in a lar (care home), a centro de dia (day centre) or in someone's home. This deck has the people and places, daily care, health and safety, the routine of a shift, and how to talk to families and colleagues. Older people are addressed as o senhor / a senhora, Dona Rosa or Sr. Manuel, with the verb in the third person, so the phrases do the same.",
  "introEasy": "Do you care for older people in Portugal? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Cuidador/a de idosos",
    "Ajudante de ação direta",
    "Auxiliar de geriatria",
    "Ajudante familiar (apoio domiciliário)",
    "Cuidador/a interno/a"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "Say who you are and what you are going to do before you touch someone: «Bom dia, Dona Rosa. Sou a Amina. Vou ajudá-la a levantar-se.» Then wait for the answer.",
    "Older people are o senhor / a senhora, Dona Rosa or Sr. Manuel, with the verb in the third person: «A senhora quer mais água?» Avoid você with them. Colleagues usually say tu to each other, and soon to you.",
    "Never guess with medication. If you are not sure, ask: «Pode confirmar com a enfermeira?» Write down what happened and tell the next shift at the passagem de turno.",
    "Paid care work can require a training course (for example for ajudante de ação direta), and the rules differ between employers (IPSS, private care home, a family). Ask the employer, the Segurança Social or a union. This is general orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
